WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.350
If you want to find out how you can win this
Pennywise keychain, then stick around to the

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:05.240
end of this video.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:14.070
The moment we’ve all been waiting for since
129 minutes into the first movie has arrived

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:16.720
-- and basically 12 videos and one terrible
song later --

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:17.930
“IT: Chapter 2.

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:19.560
Happy Death Day 2U.”

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.020
It Chapter 2 is here, so let’s take a look
at the Things You Missed.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:29.730
The movie opens with a shot from the first
film: Beverly Marsh “floating” after being

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:33.840
abducted by Pennywise, which suggests that
this event has a greater significance than

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:34.840
we previously realized.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.110
We later find out that because Beverly looked
into the Deadlights as a kid, she has the

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:42.180
ability to see things that the others can
not, which is hinted at in the last scene.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.480
“I saw us, all of us together back in the
cistern.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:48.900
We were older.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.440
Like our parents ages.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.280
I just remember how we felt.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:00:58.160
How scared we were.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:01.830
I don’t think I can ever forget that.”

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:05.530
We then see an extended version of that scene
where Stan the Man asks what he looks like

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:09.260
as an adult, and if you look at Beverly’s
reaction she looks sad for a moment before

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:11.770
lying to him, and saying he’ll be the same,
but taller.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:14.600
We all know what happens to Stan…

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:15.600
No!

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.260
No, put that down.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:21.360
We then flash forward to Derry in 2016, which
is the most modern depiction of the fictional

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.390
town that appears in many of Stephen King works.

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:27.660
The Derry Canal days festival is going on,
and three anti-gay teens harrass a couple:

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:29.140
Adrian Mellon and Don Haggerty.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:31.380
The movie shows how Adrian ended up with the
“I &lt;3

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:35.010
Derry” hat, which in the book, is the item
that sets Adrian’s attacker over the edge.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:36.010
No pun intended.

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:39.490
The scene is also inspired by a real crime
that occured in the 80s while the book was

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:40.490
being written.

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:44.320
The way the three attackers act is very similar
to how Henry Bowers and his gang of bullies

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:47.880
behave in the first film -- and that may be
a clue about how the phenomenon of violence

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.310
that Mike Hanlon calls “the Derry Disease”
is now impacting a new generation of teens.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:55.600
Mellon is thrown off the bridge, and Pennywise
secretly makes his first appearance in the

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.450
background for a brief moment while Mellon
pops up for air.

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.880
In the next scene we see Mike Hanlon hearing
the news of the crime over police scanners,

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:04.479
and I noticed a photograph of his Uncle from
the first movie.

00:02:04.479 --> 00:02:07.480
The uncle takes over the role his father played
in the novel, so I think it’s reasonable

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:11.590
to assume that he probably passed away in
a similar manner during the 27 year gap, which

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:15.340
may be the reason Mike decides to stay in
Derry and investigate more about why bad things

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:17.050
happen so often in this town.

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:20.780
The next Losers Club member we meet is Bill,
who is on set filming a movie adaptation of

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:21.780
Attic Room.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.020
In the novel, The Black Rapids is the name
of the book that gets made into a movie and

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.520
Attic Room is actually Bill’s first original
screenplay.

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:31.150
The change may have been made because each
of Bill’s stories represents one of his

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:34.770
experiences in Derry, and Attic Room would
have been inspired by the events at 29 Neibolt

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:36.870
Street, which are focused on much more in
the first movie.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:40.430
They are shooting Attic Room on the Warner
Bros lot, and conversely, you can go to the

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:42.320
Warner Bros lot right now, you’ll see this.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:51.540
It's almost as if they want people to go see
this movie...

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:55.480
The director is a real life director: Peter
Bogdanovich, likely just because the director

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:57.250
of It, Andy Muschietti, is a fan.

00:02:57.250 --> 00:02:59.040
The next adult Loser we meet is Eddie.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.450
You probably noticed that his wife, Myra,
looks exactly like his Mom from the first

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:04.739
movie, but if you haven’t read the book,
you might not get the joke.

00:03:04.739 --> 00:03:08.270
Eddie realizes the woman he married is basically
exactly like his Mother when he finds an old

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:09.420
photo of his Mom.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:12.240
“He looked from Mother to Myra and back
again to Mother.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:13.390
They could have been sisters.

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:15.170
The resemblance was that close.”

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:18.950
The movie takes it a step further by literally
using the same actress for both characters.

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:22.460
In the new movie Eddie is a risk assessment
specialist for an insurance company, but the

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:25.670
fact that he gets in a car accident when he
receives the call from Mike Hanlon is a slight

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:27.970
nod to his limo driver career in the book.

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:30.220
After Richie, we meet Ben, who is now an architect.

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:33.760
He’s in a meeting with his associates where
he makes an argument for an open floor plan,

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:37.000
telling them, “if you throw up walls, it
feels like a prison, the point is to bring

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:38.000
people together.”

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.209
Whether he remembers it or not, the reason
he feels this way is because he’s felt the

00:03:41.209 --> 00:03:45.240
power of unity before, when he and his friends
came together to defeat Pennywise.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:48.360
“This is what It wants.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:51.240
It wants to divide us.

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:53.620
We were all together when we hurt it.

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:55.930
That’s why we’re still alive.”

00:03:55.930 --> 00:04:00.090
Ben also compares the office building to a
clubhouse, a hint that we would finally see

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:03.420
the clubhouse in the Barrens that was left
out of both adaptations so far.

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:07.129
When we see the exterior shot of Ben’s home
office, there is a turtle statue -- a reference

00:04:07.129 --> 00:04:11.010
to the entity known as The Turtle in the book,
which is essentially the rival of It who gives

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:13.420
the Losers Club small helping hands along
the way.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:16.310
The Turtle is referenced a few times in the
first film, and would be referenced again

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:20.919
later in Chapter 2, when a turtle can be spotted
on the desk during Ben's classroom scene.

00:04:20.919 --> 00:04:25.870
The Turtle, like It, is a cosmic entity, and
on cue, the camera tilts up to the stars,

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:29.999
perhaps a hint that the interdimensional origins
of It will be further explained in this film.

00:04:29.999 --> 00:04:33.680
The shot transitions into Stan’s living
room, where Mr. Uris is doing a bird puzzle

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:36.569
-- a little nod to Stan’s hobby in the book,
birdwatching.

00:04:36.569 --> 00:04:40.090
Stan takes his life when hearing about It’s
return, and the blood dripping off of his

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:43.729
hand transitions to a leak dripping onto Beverly,
which is exactly what happened to Bill’s

00:04:43.729 --> 00:04:47.440
drawing of Beverly in the first one, which
by the way, leaks in through three holes on

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:51.130
the ceiling -- and later in Chapter 2, the
three balls of light that represent The Deadlights

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:53.409
come down into the chamber from above.

00:04:53.409 --> 00:04:56.469
At the end of this sequence, we see what happened
to Henry Bowers, and although we don’t see

00:04:56.469 --> 00:05:00.610
how he survived the fall down the well, we
do see his arrest for the murder of his father,

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:03.599
and the guy who slaps the cuffs on him has
a name tag reading: Borton.

00:05:03.599 --> 00:05:07.339
Chief Borton plays a pretty big role in the
book, namely, interrogating the father of

00:05:07.339 --> 00:05:10.830
the Corcoran brothers after the death of one
and the disappearance of the other.

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:14.750
Adult Henry is driven into a mad fit of laughter
after seeing a red balloon floating outside

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:18.830
the asylum he now resides in, and is taken
to his room, where another connection to King’s

00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:20.780
novel can be spotted in the background.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.660
Near Henry’s bed in the room, there are
several pictures of the moon pinned up to

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.240
the wall, and I’m like 99% sure one of them
was the moon from Termina in Majora’s Mask.

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:37.939
But the reason for these moons is that in
the novel, It manipulates Henry by giving

00:05:37.940 --> 00:05:41.720
him orders from what Henry perceives to be
a voice coming from the moon.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:43.420
"Henry…

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:46.400
Henry BOWERS!"

00:05:46.419 --> 00:05:50.129
So Zombie Patrick breaks Henry out of Juniper
Hills and later that night, the adult versions

00:05:50.129 --> 00:05:51.680
of our cast are reconvening.

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:54.939
"This meeting of the Losers Club has officially
begun."

00:05:54.939 --> 00:05:57.809
When Eddie is walking in he's in the middle
of explaining something to the waitress.

00:05:57.809 --> 00:06:01.379
He says, "If I eat a cashew, I could... realistically
die."

00:06:01.379 --> 00:06:05.050
The pause before he says those last two words
foreshadows the fact that Eddie is the next

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:06.319
character to kick the bucket.

00:06:06.319 --> 00:06:09.900
The group gets to talking about Bill's career
as a writer and we find out that Attic Room

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:14.499
wasn't his first movie adaptation, a situation
that mirrors the scenario of adaptations of

00:06:14.499 --> 00:06:15.680
It in real life.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:18.499
The movie is telling us that this may not
be the first time that someone has decided

00:06:18.499 --> 00:06:22.490
to tackle King's 1986 novel, but things are
going to go differently this time around,

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:26.270
especially with everyone Bill meets telling
him they love his writing, but he needs to

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:27.270
work on his endings.

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:30.680
When they eventually get around to talking
about It, the cycle of the monster is described

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:33.119
as an echo that comes back every 27 years.

00:06:33.119 --> 00:06:36.699
I find this word choice interesting, because
one of the new forms that the creature takes

00:06:36.699 --> 00:06:40.089
is a bat, and bats of course rely on echo
communication to get around.

00:06:40.089 --> 00:06:43.270
The idea of a bats being one of the forms
is new for this version, although there is

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:46.279
a vampire form that Ben sees briefly in the
novel.

00:06:46.279 --> 00:06:49.819
Mike tries to explain the horrors that have
gone down this past summer to the others,

00:06:49.819 --> 00:06:51.889
and briefly mentions the name Lisa Albrecht.

00:06:51.889 --> 00:06:55.629
Like Chief Borton, this is another subtle
reference to the expanded events of the book,

00:06:55.629 --> 00:06:59.419
where It kills this young girl on the anniversary
of Georgie’s death to try to send a message

00:06:59.419 --> 00:07:00.419
to Mike.

00:07:00.419 --> 00:07:04.130
It manipulates fortune cookies, first to give
the clue about Stanley's death, then to use

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:07.069
their own fears against them, and we see that
the bat is one of these.

00:07:07.069 --> 00:07:11.189
Richie gets the crawling eyeball with tentacles,
an homage to his fear of a horror movie called

00:07:11.189 --> 00:07:12.270
"The Crawling Eye."

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:15.830
Mike's cookie hatches into a bird, a connection
to the giant bird that attacked him in the

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:18.419
book and a bird that attacked him in his crib
as an infant.

00:07:18.419 --> 00:07:21.809
Beverly's spurts out blood, a reminder of
her bathroom sink that spouts blood that only

00:07:21.809 --> 00:07:22.960
kids can see.

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.350
Then there's this mutated insect with a baby's
head.

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:28.490
This is a combination of Eddie's problem in
the book where he can't get rid of the crickets

00:07:28.490 --> 00:07:32.560
in the basement, and the problem of Infertility,
all seven members of the Losers Club are unable

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:34.440
to conceive a baby as adults.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:36.800
"Any little crumbsnatchers for you Richie?"

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:39.280
"No, none that I know of."

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:42.680
While everyone is still undecided on if they
want to stay and fight, It makes the next

00:07:42.689 --> 00:07:46.110
move, luring a young girl from the bleachers
of a little league game using a lightning

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:48.889
bug, a form that again, could be connected
to deadlights.

00:07:48.889 --> 00:07:52.279
The girl’s name, Victoria, is does not line
up with one of the victims in the book, but

00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:56.360
may be a little homage to the original It,
the 1990 TV mini-series.

00:07:57.020 --> 00:07:57.920
“Who are you.”

00:07:58.380 --> 00:07:59.940
“I’m Vicky Burrows.”

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:04.039
The encounter is very similar to the Georgie
scene in Chapter 1, in that Pennywise lures

00:08:04.039 --> 00:08:07.619
her in with something she wants, in this case
removing the scar from her face.

00:08:07.619 --> 00:08:11.499
He says he’s going to do it on a count of
three, but the count ends after reaching 2,

00:08:11.499 --> 00:08:13.919
much like how It Ends after Chapter 2.

00:08:13.919 --> 00:08:17.509
That evening, Mike takes Bill to the library
and in the background there is a circus poster

00:08:17.509 --> 00:08:21.439
with Pennywise on it, though it’s not clear
to me yet if that is part of It influencing

00:08:21.439 --> 00:08:24.089
the town, or part of Mike’s research into
Derry.

00:08:24.089 --> 00:08:27.960
I did spot a book that Mike had been looking
into on his desk; A History of Old Derry by

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:29.270
Branson Buddinger.

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:32.700
This is the same book that Ben checks out
in the library in the first one, and the author

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:34.460
is a character from the novel It.

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:39.630
Buddinger, who had died in 1950, was a historian
who Mike learns of from a former Derry librarian

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:42.340
who tries persuading Mike not to look into
Derry’s past.

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:45.710
After publishing the book, A History of Old
Derry, which exposed the violent pattern of

00:08:45.710 --> 00:08:49.750
the town’s past, Buddinger was found dead
by his own hand, but like all of the tragedies

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:53.820
in this town, something prevents the story
from picking up significant news coverage.’

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:57.080
Another book that I noticed in Mike’s study
was called Pillar in the Sky -- it may be

00:08:57.080 --> 00:09:00.930
in reference to The Dark Tower, a series of
Stephen King books that expand on the mythology

00:09:00.930 --> 00:09:02.710
of deities such as the Turtle.

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:04.850
Mike has Bill perform the smoke hole ceremony.

00:09:04.850 --> 00:09:07.860
In the book, the seven of them all do this
as kids, but only Richie and Mike have the

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:09.300
vision about the arrival of It.

00:09:09.300 --> 00:09:13.300
But in the movie, Bill is able to see the
abstract vision of the three beacons of light,

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:17.020
which match what Beverly saw in the first
movie, and It which shapeshifts into a couple

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:21.140
different forms, such as Mike’s giant bird,
and the spider creature that they see at the

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:22.140
end.

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:24.970
After seeing this vision, and Beverly telling
of the nightmares she had of their deaths,

00:09:24.970 --> 00:09:29.150
the group decides to stay and fight back against
It, and Mike takes them back to The Barrens,

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:30.970
where they come across their old clubhouse.

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:34.360
In the flashback, they wear surgical caps
while in the clubhouse to prevent spiders

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:37.530
from getting in their hair, to which Richie
asks if “they’re really going to be afraid

00:09:37.530 --> 00:09:38.590
of spiders.”

00:09:38.590 --> 00:09:42.610
In the end, overcoming their fear of the spider
form of Pennywise is how they ultimately defeat

00:09:42.610 --> 00:09:43.610
It.

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:46.690
Mike gives them the mission to find the artifacts
of their past throughout Derry, during which

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:50.300
there are more flashbacks, like Beverly’s
abusive father covering her with her late

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:51.300
mother’s perfume.

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:54.260
The scene reminds me a lot of this one line
from the book, which Beverly describes her

00:09:54.260 --> 00:09:56.850
dad’s increasing willingness to raise his
hands against her.

00:09:56.850 --> 00:10:00.370
“It was as if there was a smell they made
between them, a smell that wasn’t there

00:10:00.370 --> 00:10:03.890
when she was in the apartment alone, one that
had never been there when they were in it

00:10:03.890 --> 00:10:05.430
together—not until this summer.

00:10:05.430 --> 00:10:07.250
And when Mom was gone it was worse.

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:11.360
If there was a smell, some smell, then he
knew it too, because Bev saw less and less

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:13.650
of him as the hot weather wore on.”

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:16.750
Beverly goes back to her old apartment, where
Mrs. Kersh now lives.

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:20.240
She notices something is off when she spots
the holes on Mrs. Kersh’s chest, a lasting

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:23.720
injuring from where Ben stabbed the monster
with the fire poker 27 years ago.

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:24.840
Roll it.

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:26.240
"AHAHA!"

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:27.700
"AHHH!"

00:10:32.020 --> 00:10:33.100
"Kill it!"

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:36.620
The next thing Beverly notices is the photo
of Mrs. Kersh and her fodder.

00:10:36.621 --> 00:10:39.140
“My fodder joined the circus.”

00:10:39.140 --> 00:10:43.530
...and aside from the familiar facial features
of this man, there’s also a familiar looking

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:45.360
trailer in the background.

00:10:55.460 --> 00:10:59.420
The next flashback would come from Richie,
who revisits the old movie theater arcade,

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:03.960
where an interesting detail can be found.

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:12.860
The old movie theater has been closed down,
with the marquee now simply reading, “thanks

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:13.860
for the memories”,

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:15.620
"...even though they weren't so great!"

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:18.770
which is kind of a shame because they could
have put up posters for Warner Bros movies

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:21.640
from 2016 like Lights Out and The Conjuring
2.

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:24.950
But instead, there’s just an old left behind
poster for You’ve Got Mail, which tells

00:11:24.950 --> 00:11:29.490
us that the theater probably closed in 1998,
nine years after the initial battle with Pennywise.

00:11:29.490 --> 00:11:33.300
Also, this is the second Meg Ryan reference
so far, the first being Adrian Mellon telling

00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:35.950
the bullies that “Meg Ryan wants her wig
back” in the opening.

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:39.470
Richie flashes back to a scene where he flees
from Henry Bowers and takes a safe haven in

00:11:39.470 --> 00:11:43.580
City Center, where he is attacked by the Paul
Bunyan statue who has bats flying out of his

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:46.220
mouth, which explains the aforementioned fortune
cookie with the bat.

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:50.400
Then, in the present, Richie reads a flyer
for his own funeral, which is either a callback

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:54.080
to the missing poster from the first movie,
or the poster for Richie Tozier’s All Dead

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:55.800
Rock Show from the book.

00:11:55.800 --> 00:11:56.800
Or both.

00:11:56.800 --> 00:11:57.800
Could be both.

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:02.350
Meanwhile, Bill goes into a shop called Secondhand
Rose when he sees his old bike, Silver.

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:06.950
The shopkeep is none other than Stephen King
himself, which provides an interesting conversation,

00:12:06.950 --> 00:12:10.480
given that Bill as a character is already
kind of an extension of Stephen King, it’s

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:14.340
almost as if King is having a conversation
with an older version of himself, something

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:18.090
that actually happens during the climax of
the movie during the Ritual of Chud.

00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:21.690
King also has a copy of The Black Rapids -- the
fictional Bill Denbrough book I mentioned

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:22.690
earlier.

00:12:22.690 --> 00:12:25.510
Bill tells the shopkeep that his old bike
was “fast enough to beat the devil”, and

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:28.880
the line is a direct reference to the chapter
title, “Bill Denbrough Beats The Devil”,

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:31.330
which is brought back as the tagline of the
book’s epilogue.

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:34.590
Next, Bill visits his old house, where a new
family now lives.

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:36.320
The son is a kid who rides a skateboard.

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:40.570
The bottom of his board displays the carpet
pattern seen outside room 237 in The Shining.

00:12:40.570 --> 00:12:42.980
The skateboard kid does appear in the novel
briefly.

00:12:42.980 --> 00:12:46.340
Bill tells him to be careful, to which he
replies, ‘you can’t be careful on a skateboard’,

00:12:46.340 --> 00:12:49.120
a line that continues to come up in Bill’s
mind throughout the book.

00:12:49.120 --> 00:12:53.060
In the movie, Bill tells him to be careful,
and the kid just looks back… maybe I’m

00:12:53.060 --> 00:12:56.210
looking into it too much, but maybe this is
Muschietti telling us that things are going

00:12:56.210 --> 00:12:57.840
to go differently from here on out.

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:01.200
And after seeing this thing, it’s hard to
say I can blame him.

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:03.900
Ben searches for his artifact back at the
high school.

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:06.340
“Neeeeerrrrrrrrrrdddddddddddddd!!!!!!!!!!”

00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:09.990
He flashes back to a scene that took place
while the kids were back in school, after

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:11.830
this fight, but before defeating It.

00:13:11.830 --> 00:13:15.370
It’s interesting how both movies added a
scene of It taking the form of Beverly in

00:13:15.370 --> 00:13:19.250
order to trick Ben, as far as I can remember,
there’s nothing like that in the novel.

00:13:19.250 --> 00:13:22.930
In this version however, before Ben kisses
her, she turns into a monster with a flaming

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:26.490
head -- I believe this is IT taunting him
about the poem he had written her, turning

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:29.760
the line “your hair is winter fire” into
something literal.

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:33.370
Also worth noting, this could be a reference
to another creepy clown, Sweet Tooth, from

00:13:33.370 --> 00:13:37.000
the Twisted Metal franchise -- however I don’t
think it was intentional because the scenes

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:41.100
where Ben is a kid take place in 1989 and
Twisted Metal wouldn’t come out until 1995.

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:45.250
Also, Ben saves himself by locking himself
inside his locker, a scene that is very similar

00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:47.360
to Creepy Clown School Lockdown: Epidemic.

00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:49.500
I might need to have my lawyers look into
this one.

00:13:49.510 --> 00:13:53.100
And perhaps even more suspicious than that,
is in the next sequence where Eddie stops

00:13:53.100 --> 00:13:57.160
in to pick up his prescription at Center Street
Drug, they somehow have me in the background

00:13:57.160 --> 00:13:58.540
browsing the aisles?!

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:03.480
Oh wait, that’s actually a cameo from the
director, Andy Muschietti, which completes

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:06.870
the trifecta of cameos: Bogdanovich, King
and Muschietti.

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:09.850
If you were wondering if the woman behind
the counter is Greta Keene, the same girl

00:14:09.850 --> 00:14:13.170
who writes ‘LOSER’ on Eddie’s cast,
she’s still wearing the same necklace with

00:14:13.170 --> 00:14:16.640
the letter G. Eddie’s flashback takes him
to a scene where he’s lured into the basement

00:14:16.640 --> 00:14:19.490
of the pharmacy when he hears his mother’s
voice crying for help.

00:14:19.490 --> 00:14:23.100
He looks behind the curtain to discover his
mother tied up to a chair with the Leper coming

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:24.100
for her.

00:14:24.100 --> 00:14:27.130
Though this is only an illusion created by
It, he is unable to save her, but this is

00:14:27.130 --> 00:14:30.810
a visual setup for when Eddie overcomes his
fear of the horrors that are beyond the curtain

00:14:30.810 --> 00:14:34.740
later on as an adult, when he punctures through
the shower curtain to kill his attacker, Henry

00:14:34.740 --> 00:14:35.460
Bowers.

00:14:35.460 --> 00:14:38.740
When I covered the first movie, one thing
I pointed out was how Tim Curry’s Pennywise

00:14:38.740 --> 00:14:42.200
from the mini-series adaptation of It makes
a small cameo in the clown room.

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:45.580
Chapter 2 continues that tradition when Bill
makes his way through the haunted house in

00:14:45.580 --> 00:14:47.460
an attempt to rescue the skateboard kid.

00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:51.350
These swinging clown pendulums share the yellow
suit, purple vest, parted tufts of red hair

00:14:51.350 --> 00:14:54.960
and red rubber nose that can be found on Curry’s
Pennywise, whereas none of these features

00:14:54.960 --> 00:14:58.080
can be found on the new design for Bill Skarsgård’s
Pennywise.

00:14:58.080 --> 00:15:01.810
When he gets into the hall of mirrors, Pennywise
appears for less than a second in the background

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:03.660
during one of the strobes of light.

00:15:03.660 --> 00:15:07.480
After being unable to save the skateboard
kid, Bill loses it and goes alone to the house

00:15:07.480 --> 00:15:11.330
where they had encountered It so many years
before: 29 Neibolt Street.

00:15:11.330 --> 00:15:14.960
Just like when Bill foolishly ran off to face
the threat alone as a child, his friends give

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:16.290
chase to come aid him.

00:15:16.290 --> 00:15:19.740
They go inside, where they come up against
a bit of a bridge between something that happens

00:15:19.740 --> 00:15:22.830
in the book and the second coming of something
that happens in the first movie.

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:26.200
In the first film, Pennywise comes out of
this fridge to attack them...

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:42.080
"Time to float!"

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:46.140
...and in the book, they are having a discussion
in the library, and It uses the form of Stan’s

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:48.500
head inside of a mini-fridge to taunt and
frighten them.

00:15:56.520 --> 00:15:57.520
"Stan!"

00:15:57.760 --> 00:15:58.760
"Sorry I'm late.

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:00.260
Well let's see who's here."

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:03.870
So when The Amazing Spiderstan comes out of
the fridge, it does a good job of bringing

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:05.110
those two worlds together.

00:16:05.110 --> 00:16:08.480
The idea of someone’s head turning into
a spider, and the line that Richie says when

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:11.860
he sees it, is also a reference to John Carpenter’s
The Thing.

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:30.440
“You’ve gotta be ****ing kidding.”

00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:35.870
We also see the H on Ben’s abdomen, that
Henry carved in the first movie, reappear,

00:16:35.870 --> 00:16:38.460
this time spelling out the words: Home At
Last.

00:16:38.460 --> 00:16:41.721
After another run in with the monstrous version
of Mrs. Kersh, who apparently didn’t get

00:16:41.721 --> 00:16:43.360
the memo about It’s catchphrase.

00:16:43.520 --> 00:16:44.520
“You’ll float too.”

00:16:44.740 --> 00:16:45.680
No, not that one.

00:16:45.680 --> 00:16:49.180
“You lied, and I died!”

00:16:49.180 --> 00:16:50.320
No, the other one.

00:16:50.330 --> 00:16:51.350
“Time to float!”

00:16:51.350 --> 00:16:52.370
Yeah, that one.

00:16:52.370 --> 00:16:56.220
They end up finding a manhole in the cistern
that leads them deeper into the sewers and

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:59.920
this is where they all must perform the smoke
hole ritual together, in scene that’s filled

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:01.560
with even more secrets.

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:12.740
The matches that Mike uses to start the fire
that begins the smoke hole ritual are from

00:17:12.740 --> 00:17:16.059
Jade of the Orient, the Chinese restaurant
that they meet at for the reunion.

00:17:16.059 --> 00:17:19.611
The Ritual of Chud begins, and this isn’t
a Thing You Missed, but I just feel the need

00:17:19.611 --> 00:17:23.539
to point out I ****ing called it when I uploaded
this video on May 25th.

00:17:23.539 --> 00:17:24.539
Roll it.

00:17:24.539 --> 00:17:28.519
“I just think that they have to make a cooler
looking arachnid than the one from 1990, maybe

00:17:28.519 --> 00:17:32.799
instead of a giant spider, it’s more of
a half clown, half spider alien hybrid.”

00:17:32.799 --> 00:17:35.480
Now, I know what you’re thinking, I should
just mic drop and walk away.

00:17:35.480 --> 00:17:38.510
But the mic is up there and I still have a
few more Things You Missed.

00:17:38.510 --> 00:17:41.780
And I’m actually borrowing this mic at the
moment, I don’t really want to risk breaking

00:17:41.780 --> 00:17:42.780
it.

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:44.450
The point is, you owe me a like on this video
or something.

00:17:44.450 --> 00:17:47.990
But anyway, each of the Losers is thrown into
their own personal scenarios where they must

00:17:47.990 --> 00:17:49.220
overcome Pennywise.

00:17:49.220 --> 00:17:52.580
For Beverly, she torn into the bathroom stall
where she was bullied as a young girl, or

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:54.340
at least some version of it.

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:57.720
The word smokehole can briefly be seen written
on the wall, and the blood that nearly drowns

00:17:57.720 --> 00:18:01.289
her is an extreme version of the blood that
came out of her father’s bathroom sink.

00:18:01.289 --> 00:18:04.330
In the first movie, it’s a symbol for her
struggles with reaching the age of puberty,

00:18:04.330 --> 00:18:07.779
but in this film, it’s more of a reminder
of the abuse she endured as a child, and this

00:18:07.779 --> 00:18:11.070
is solidified by the fact that the characters
that poke their heads into her bathroom stall

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:15.350
are the three predatory characters that objectified
her, her father, the pharmacist and Henry

00:18:15.350 --> 00:18:18.820
Bowers, who screams, “Here’s Johnny!”
as he pokes his head around the door.

00:18:18.820 --> 00:18:20.540
“Here’s Johnny!”

00:18:20.549 --> 00:18:25.120
I did a whole video on the connection between
The Shining and It, which you can watch here,

00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:27.090
or find the link in the description at the
end of this video.

00:18:27.090 --> 00:18:30.860
Eddie and Richie encounter the set of three
doors that terrified them in the first movie,

00:18:30.860 --> 00:18:34.149
and behind the first one, there's a pair
of legs unattached to a body.

00:18:34.149 --> 00:18:37.539
If you remember in Chapter One, Bill and Richie
open a door and see the severed upper body

00:18:37.539 --> 00:18:40.950
of their missing classmate, Betty Ripsom,
so I think this kind of shows, that they are

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:42.309
in the inverse of that world.

00:18:42.309 --> 00:18:46.059
I also found it kind of funny that they actually
didn’t get the same actress back for this

00:18:46.059 --> 00:18:49.490
sequel, so the new actress is credited as
Betty Ripsom’s legs.

00:18:49.490 --> 00:18:52.820
After everyone makes it out of their personal
battles with It, Eddie finds his bravery and

00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:56.600
attacks the monster, which retaliates and
seriously maims Eddie, leaving a smudge of

00:18:56.600 --> 00:18:57.779
clown makeup on his cheek.

00:18:57.779 --> 00:19:01.309
It you look closely, you can see the word:
IT, though I probably don’t have the footage

00:19:01.309 --> 00:19:02.980
for this, so you’ll have to go back and
see the movie.

00:19:05.850 --> 00:19:09.140
When the Losers get an advantage over It,
the creature cycles among many of the forms,

00:19:09.159 --> 00:19:12.200
and you can tell that It’s losing It’s
touch when it briefly takes the form of the

00:19:12.200 --> 00:19:15.330
woman in the painting, which was Stan’s
fear, and he hasn’t been around since like

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:16.730
15 minutes into the movie.

00:19:16.730 --> 00:19:20.580
To be fair though, that thing is pretty freaky
regardless of if you’re Stan or not.

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:23.961
After losing Eddie, the others are able to
come together and finally put an end to the

00:19:23.961 --> 00:19:27.890
thing that has caused the horrors around Derry
for all these years, and they escape from

00:19:27.890 --> 00:19:29.860
the Neibolt house just before it collapses.

00:19:29.860 --> 00:19:33.950
This is a scaled down version of the way that
the book ends, where an epic flood destroys

00:19:33.950 --> 00:19:37.299
Derry and symbolically purges all the evil
that had taken place there.

00:19:37.299 --> 00:19:40.159
So this was the one thing I was a little disappointed
with for this movie.

00:19:40.159 --> 00:19:41.860
That storm scene would have been wild.

00:19:41.860 --> 00:19:45.299
But as I mentioned way back at the beginning
of this video, it was well established that

00:19:45.299 --> 00:19:48.929
the movie was going to do something different,
because Bill Denbrough, who kind of represents

00:19:48.929 --> 00:19:53.590
Stephen King, is criticized for his story
endings, by his wife, Peter Bogdanovich, and

00:19:53.590 --> 00:19:56.950
basically everyone he meets along the way,
so his next book, that we see him writing

00:19:56.950 --> 00:20:00.950
in the epilogue, has a more positive ending,
and It Chapter 2 follows suit.

00:20:00.950 --> 00:20:02.509
The town is not destroyed.

00:20:02.509 --> 00:20:04.429
The evil is conclusively vanquished.

00:20:04.429 --> 00:20:08.399
And the remaining members of the Losers Club
don’t lose their memories again when they

00:20:08.399 --> 00:20:09.399
leave Derry.

00:20:09.399 --> 00:20:13.210
Bill Denbrough does once again, beat the Devil,
but this time, it’s a personal demon that

00:20:13.210 --> 00:20:14.389
he’s overcoming.

00:20:14.389 --> 00:20:18.889
The ancient evil that for so long haunted
Derry, was now gone… but you are still gonna

00:20:18.889 --> 00:20:22.460
have a chance to have your own evil entity,
in the form of a keychain!

